WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.598
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.598 --> 00:00:05.580
we take a look at a service that
powers billion-user systems--

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.570
Bigtable.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.520
Will we set enough
places for performance?

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.480
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:12.187 --> 00:00:13.770
When it comes to
performance at scale,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.140
there's very few
products that truly

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.740
represent that term the way
that Cloud Bigtable does.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.420
To date, Bigtable
is the technology

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.200
behind the majority
of Google products,

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.070
including Gmail, Maps,
and YouTube, each of which

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.650
serves multi-billion
active users.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.350
And since Bigtable is part
of Google Cloud Platform,

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.147
it's available to use in
your applications as well.

00:00:34.147 --> 00:00:35.730
But before you run
off into the weeds,

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.540
switching over your
database to use it,

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.930
we need to take a look at
how things work and show

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.520
how it should and
shouldn't be used.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.220
So let's take a
look under the hood.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.830
As a massive generalization,
Cloud Bigtable

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.530
can be described as three
systems working together.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.440
First, you have a
front end server pool.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.810
Second, you have a
set of compute nodes

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.050
that handle connections,
a.k.a. the Bigtable cluster.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.260
And thirdly, you have a
scalable back end of storage.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.069
Now, see, a client request goes
through a front end server pool

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:08.130
before they are sent to
the Cloud Bigtable node.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.810
Therefore, each
node in the cluster

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.240
handles a subset of requests
for the entire system,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.820
and the front ends will handle
balancing these connections,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.070
depending on the type of
action that the connection is

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.520
interested in and what table
it's wanting to work on.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.100
To store the underlying
data in each of your tables,

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.300
Cloud Bigtable shards the
data into multiple tablets

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.580
where each tablet
contains a contiguous

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.930
range of rows within the table.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.140
I know-- a lot of vernacular.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.520
And here's the important thing
when it comes to tablets.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.305
They can be reassigned to
different nodes in your cluster

00:01:38.305 --> 00:01:41.460
on demand, allowing Bigtable
to scale and rebalance

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.850
seamlessly as your
usage patterns change.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.240
For example, as reads and writes
to a specific tablet increase,

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.212
Cloud Bigtable might
split it in two

00:01:50.212 --> 00:01:53.700
in order to isolate hot rows
and improve performance.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.220
From here, you can clearly
see that Bigtable does not

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.260
act like other
relational databases.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.960
And more to the point, while
it scales amazingly well

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.150
and isolates
hotspots gracefully,

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.250
that doesn't make it a perfect
use case for every scenario.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.780
See, Cloud Bigtable excels at
handling very large amounts

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.840
of data, like terabytes,
over relatively long periods

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.550
of time, like days.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.110
This is because
Bigtable needs time

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.300
to learn your access
patterns, and the data

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.750
needs time to be large enough
to warrant usage across all

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.400
of the nodes in your cluster.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.040
This makes it a perfect fit
for things like time series

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.960
data, marketing
data, low latency

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.350
serving, financial data,
IoT data, and graph data--

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:35.700
basically, systems with lots
of data and contiguous usage.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.300
Now, if your data needs are
small, like less than 300 gigs,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.814
or if your interactions last
for short periods of time,

00:02:41.814 --> 00:02:43.980
then check out the article
linked in the description

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.990
below where we list some
other technologies that

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.630
might be a better fit
for your application.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.640
Likewise, don't
forget to subscribe

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.000
to our YouTube channel for more
great Cloud Performance Atlas

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:54.629
content.

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:56.170
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.690
every millisecond counts.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.340
